  "query_preview": "..\n\nA cold front continues to drop south across the area early this \nmorning. This will help gradually diminish the warmer/muggy air mass \nacross the region, with breezy west to northwest winds ushering in \nlower dewpoints this afternoon. As the front moves through it is not \nout of the question that a few spotty showers develop. However, \nbetter lift and upper-level forcing arrives later this afternoon \nwhen the main upper-level wave tracks through the region. Maintained \nchance PoPs for this afternoon and evening as an area of \nfrontogenesis between roughly 800-700 mb sharpens upon the arrival \nof the upper level wave and increasing dCVA. Not expecting any \nsignificant rainfall with any shower activity that develops this \nafternoon. \n\nTonight through Monday night, high pressure takes over across the \nregion and upper-level ridging builds back into the region. This \npattern will bring a taste of fall to the region. As a much drier \nair mass moves overhead tonight, the combination of clearing skies \nand light winds will allow temperatures to drop well into the 40s. \nGiven the crisp, cool night expected, we will likely see some patchy \nfog develop in/around lakes and rivers. Highs on Monday are likely \nto top out around 70 with light winds and mostly clear skies. Cloud \ncover increases Monday night as high pressure departs to our east \nand the leading edge of warm air advection begins to move in from \nthe west ahead of the next system expected to impact the region.\n\nThis system, while the strong surface low is progged to remain well \nnorth of the region, will bring impacts starting on Tuesday as \nstrong warm air and moisture advection return to the region. Highs \nare forecast to climb back up into the 80s with dewpoints in the \nupper 60s/near 70. This round of muggy conditions will be brief as a \nslow moving cold front drops south across the area late Tuesday. \nFlow along this frontal boundary becomes increasingly unidirectional \nand zonal, which will slow the southward progression of the front \nthrough mid to late week. The question remains where the front is \nwhen it stalls out. Is it over southeast Michigan or south of the \nstate line. Models are also trying to bring another shortwave in \nfrom the west, which would bring another focus for showers and \nthunderstorms late next week. There are plenty of details that will \nbe refined as we get closer, but Tuesday through late next week \ncurrently feature numerous chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nincluding the potential for training precipitation.",
